              title: "Access to St Elen's: a working landscape, not a car park",
              source: "Informe del St Elen's Trust, consultado en la biblioteca de Palmer Road. Texto adaptado.",
              text: "Standing on the ridge above Llynfaen, you see the priory before you see the village. The ruin occupies a shelf of land between the estuary and a line of [[dry-stone walls|muros de piedra en seco]] that have marked the same fields since the sixteenth century. St Elen's has been a [[World Heritage site|sitio Patrimonio de la Humanidad]] since 2004. It is also, and this is the point this report has to hold together, a working landscape: sheep still graze the approach, and a public right of way still crosses the inner court.<br/><br/>Footfall has doubled in five years. The visitor centre, opened in 2018, was designed for 40,000 people a year; last year 83,000 came through the gate. Most arrived by car. The approach road is a single track with passing places, and on summer Saturdays the queue reaches back to the A-road. Local residents have written to the Trust in terms that are hard to argue with. Something has to change.<br/><br/>Two options are on the table. The first is a car park of 180 spaces on the field immediately west of the visitor centre. It would be cheap to build and it would be full by half past ten. After having walked the field with the county ecologist, we cannot recommend it. The field is a sensitive habitat for lapwing, and the proposed route of the access lane would cut a public right of way that has been in continuous use since the tithe maps. Building there would solve a traffic problem by creating a conservation one.<br/><br/>The second option is a park-and-ride on the north side of the village, with a shuttle every twenty minutes. The shuttle would add twelve minutes to the visit. It would also leave the coast path, the inner court and the lapwing field untouched. Rather than pouring more cars into the shelf of land that makes the priory what it is, we would move the cars away from it. Gwen Pryce, who has guided here for eighteen years, put it more plainly on the site visit: «People come for the silence. You cannot have the silence and the car park.»<br/><br/>There had been a third proposal, briefly: a bypass looping south of the estuary. It looked, on the first maps, as if it would solve everything. After having modelled the earthworks, the engineers withdrew it. The southern loop would have destroyed two kilometres of dry-stone wall and taken a bite out of the salt marsh. In the interests of the listed landscape, that option is closed.<br/><br/>This Trust's recommendation is therefore the park-and-ride, with a view to reducing congestion at the gate without spending the landscape that people have come to see. We accept that some visitors will complain about the shuttle. We also accept that a World Heritage site is not a supermarket, and that getting there is part of what the place means. Protecting open spaces is not a slogan we print on a leaflet. It is the reason the priory is still visible from the ridge.<br/><br/>One further point, because it keeps being raised in the consultation: satellite navigation. The last junction at the church still offers a farm track that is not a public road. After having put up a sign, we still meet drivers who have followed the machine rather than the map. Green travel, in this valley, starts with not sending a car down a lane that cannot take it. The Trust will keep the sign, keep the shuttle, and keep saying the same thing on the guided tour: the landscape is the destination, not the obstacle between you and a photograph. The sheep, for their part, have not been consulted, and they will still be there if we get this right.",
